    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make an issue of the panel being made up entirely of men, nor does it provide any actionable signal in this context.**\n\nHere is an analysis of why, along with a perspective on what more likely influenced the outcome:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel’s Gender Composition Isn’t an Actionable Factor\n* **No apparent gender bias in the outcome:** Both you and the candidate who was appointed are men. Legally and practically, an all-male panel cannot easily be cited as evidence of gender discrimination against another male applicant.\n* **Industry demographics:** In senior structural engineering, executive leadership and department heads have historically been predominantly male. While modern HR best practice encourages diverse interview panels, having four senior male engineers or directors on an interview panel is still very common and does not inherently imply impropriety.\n* **Risk of raising it:** Raising questions about the panel's gender composition as a male candidate who was passed over for another male candidate will likely come across as grasping for grievances. It risks burning bridges with a consultancy that considered you a top-three candidate.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What the Outcome More Likely Points To\nFor a **Head of Group** position, the criteria differ substantially from senior or principal engineer roles:\n\n* **Commercial focus vs. technical depth:** The announcement specifically highlighted **\"excellent client relationships.\"** At the department-head level, firms often prioritize business development, client retention, fee generation, and work-winning capability over years of design calculations or technical leadership. Someone with 8 years of experience who has proven commercial leverage or brings an active client book can often edge out someone with 15 years of technical delivery.\n* **Internal familiarity or network:** It is worth considering whether the successful candidate had preexisting relationships with key clients, the firm itself, or members of the panel.\n* **Remuneration and runway:** While less commonly stated openly, a candidate with 8 years of experience may have aligned better with their compensation budget or been viewed as having a different long-term trajectory within the firm.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Lack of Feedback After Two Weeks\n* **Standard corporate delays:** Two weeks without a response to a written feedback request is common. Leadership panels often disband back into billable project work immediately after an appointment.\n* **Liability concerns:** Many corporate HR departments actively discourage or delay providing detailed written explanations to unsuccessful final-round applicants to minimize legal exposure.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n1. **Send a polite, low-friction follow-up:** If you want feedback, follow up with the recruiter or hiring lead. Frame it purely around personal development rather than an audit of their decision:\n   > *\"I understand you are likely busy following the appointment, but as a final-round candidate, I would greatly appreciate 10–15 minutes of verbal feedback on where I can strengthen my profile for future group head opportunities.\"*\n2. **Opt for a phone call over a written explanation:** Hiring managers are far more candid and responsive over a 10-minute phone call than when asked to put formal assessments in writing.\n3. **Keep the relationship positive:** You made the final shortlist of three for a senior leadership post. Leaving a professional, gracious impression leaves the door open for future leadership roles or partnership opportunities.\n",
